// Module 4 — Scaling Patterns: scaling up and out, autoscaling, the edge, regions and service boundaries.
(function (T) {

  T({
    id: '4.1', mod: '4', title: 'Horizontal vs vertical scaling', tag: 'A bigger machine or more machines',
    analogy: 'Moving house with a bigger van or with more vans. A bigger van needs no planning, but there is a largest van, and if it breaks down the move stops. More vans need drivers who agree who carries what; past some number, the time spent coordinating outgrows the extra capacity.',
    points: [
      'Scaling up (vertical) buys a bigger machine: no code changes, but there is a largest machine, and it is still one failure domain. Scaling out (horizontal) adds machines: capacity in small steps, and one failure costs 1/N of capacity, but the work has to be divisible.',
      'The precondition for scaling out is statelessness: no session or data kept in a server\'s memory or disk between requests, with state held in a database, cache or other backing service. Then any instance can serve any request and can be replaced at will. Sticky sessions break this.',
      'Coordination limits scale-out. If a fraction α of the work is serialised (a lock, a single writer), speed-up approaches 1/α however many nodes you add: 5% serial caps it at 20×. The Universal Scalability Law adds a coherency cost β for keeping nodes consistent; with β > 0, throughput peaks at N = √((1 − α) ÷ β) and then falls.',
      'Small instances make failure cheaper and headroom easier to plan. Google SRE practice is N + 2: enough capacity to serve peak with one instance down for maintenance and another failing. With two big machines, one failure halves capacity.',
      'Databases usually scale up first, because they hold shared state that is hard to split (topic 2.1). Stack Overflow wrote in 2016 that it ran on two SQL Server clusters, each with one primary taking almost all the load, on servers with 384 GB and 768 GB of RAM, behind 9 primary web servers.',
      'Scaling well is not the same as being fast. McSherry, Isard and Murray (2015) measured a single thread on a laptop running 20 PageRank iterations on a 1.5-billion-edge graph in 275 s; published systems on 128 cores took from 249 s to 857 s. Compare against one good machine before distributing.'
    ],
    pitfall: 'Adding instances to a tier that keeps sessions in local memory, or whose work is serialised on one lock or one database row. New instances lose users\' sessions or just add contention, and throughput stays flat or falls.',
    say: 'The API tier keeps no local state, so it scales out behind the load balancer, sized N + 2 for peak. The database I scale up first: one large primary with a replica goes a long way, as Stack Overflow showed, and I would shard only when writes or data outgrow the biggest machine available.',
    refs: [
      ['azure-sharding', 'Scaling up postpones the limit — “To postpone these limitations temporarily, you can scale vertically by adding disk capacity, processing power, memory, and network connections.”'],
      ['aws-wa-rel-design-principles', 'Scaling out for availability — “Replace one large resource with multiple small resources to reduce the impact of a single failure on the overall workload.”'],
      ['aws-wa-stateless', 'Stateless lets any instance serve — “another benefit of stateless applications is that they can scale horizontally because any of the available compute resources (such as EC2 instances and AWS Lambda functions) can service any request.”'],
      ['12factor-processes', 'No sticky sessions — “Sticky sessions are a violation of twelve-factor and should never be used or relied upon.”'],
      ['gunther-usl', 'Contention alone sets a ceiling (Amdahl\'s case, β = 0) — “Put differently, the scalability curve simply approaches a ceiling at 1/α (the horizontal dashed line in the plots).”'],
      ['gunther-usl', 'Coherency makes it fall — “A non-zero value of β is associated with measured throughput that goes retrograde, i.e., decreases with increasing load or platform configuration.”'],
      ['gunther-usl', 'Where the peak is, Nmax = √((1 − α)/β) — “The fitted coefficients in eqn.(3) then provide an estimate of the user load at which the maximum scalability will occur”'],
      ['sre-best-practices', 'N + 2 — “Provision to handle a simultaneous planned and unplanned outage, without making the user experience unacceptable”'],
      ['stackoverflow-arch-2016', 'Scale-up database (Stack Overflow, 2016) — “Each of these clusters has 1 master (taking almost all of the load) and 1 replica in New York.”'],
      ['cost-hotos15', 'COST — “The COST of a given platform for a given problem is the hardware configuration required before the platform outperforms a competent single-threaded implementation.”']
    ],
    cards: [
      ['What must be true of a service before you can scale it out freely?', 'It must be stateless between requests: sessions and data live in a backing service, not in the instance\'s memory or disk. Then any instance can take any request, and instances can be added, removed or replaced without users noticing.'],
      ['What do Amdahl\'s law and the USL say about adding nodes?', 'Amdahl: a serial fraction α caps speed-up at 1/α. The USL adds a coherency term β (the cost of keeping nodes consistent); when β > 0, throughput peaks at N = √((1 − α)/β) and adding nodes beyond that makes it fall.'],
      ['When is scaling up the better first move?', 'For stateful components that are hard to split, above all the database, while a bigger machine is still available: it needs no code change and avoids the permanent complexity of sharding. Stateless tiers scale out instead.']
    ],
    quiz: [
      ['Profiling shows 5% of each request\'s work is serialised on a global lock, with no other coordination cost (α = 0.05, β = 0). Relative to one node, how much throughput do 16 nodes give?', ['About 9×', 'About 16×', 'About 15×', 'About 20×'], 'C(16) = 16 ÷ (1 + 0.05 × 15) = 16 ÷ 1.75 ≈ 9.1. 16× ignores the lock; 15× (16 × 0.95) scales the parallel share without counting the waiting; 20× is the ceiling 1/α, approached only as N grows without bound.', 'calc'],
      ['A load test fits the Universal Scalability Law with α = 0.02 and β = 0.0005. Beyond roughly how many nodes does adding more make throughput fall?', ['About 44', 'About 50', 'About 1,960', 'About 6'], 'N = √((1 − α) ÷ β) = √(0.98 ÷ 0.0005) = √1,960 ≈ 44. 50 is 1/α, the contention-only ceiling on speed-up, not a node count; 1,960 forgets the square root; 6 puts α in place of 1 − α (√(0.02 ÷ 0.0005) = √40).', ['gunther-usl']],
      ['A web app runs on one server and keeps each shopping cart in that server\'s memory. Traffic grows, so 3 more servers are added behind a round-robin load balancer. What do users see?', ['Carts that empty or change as requests reach other servers', 'No change, as the balancer copies session state to new servers', 'Slower pages while carts are copied over to the new servers', 'Errors until each new server warms its cache from the others'], 'The servers are not stateless, so a request that lands on a server without that user\'s cart sees an empty one. Move session state to a shared store such as Redis. Pinning users to servers (sticky sessions) hides the problem until a server dies and takes its carts with it.', ['aws-wa-stateless', '12factor-processes']],
      ['An OLTP database runs at 70% CPU on 16 cores and 128 GB of RAM, with 400 GB of data; writes grow 30% a year. The team proposes sharding across 8 nodes now. Better first move?', ['A larger instance now; plan sharding for when the largest is near', 'Shard now, since vertical scaling will hit its ceiling eventually', 'Add read replicas, so that the write load spreads across nodes', 'Add application servers, so that each query finishes faster'], 'A 128 GB machine is far from the largest available (Stack Overflow\'s database servers had 384–768 GB in 2016, each cluster with one primary taking almost all the load), so scaling up buys years without the permanent complexity of sharding. Replicas do not take writes, and more application servers add load to the database rather than removing it.', ['azure-sharding', 'stackoverflow-arch-2016']],
      ['A 64-node cluster runs a graph job in 20 minutes and scales almost linearly from 8 to 64 nodes. A careful single-threaded program on a laptop takes 15 minutes on the same input. What does this suggest?', ['Much of its scaling spreads overhead that it adds itself', 'It is efficient, since near-linear scaling means low overhead', 'Each node is slower than a laptop core; newer hardware fixes it', 'Its speed-up curve shows it will beat one thread on more data'], 'This is the COST argument: no configuration tried beats one competent thread, so the good scaling curve measures the system parallelising its own overheads. McSherry et al. found several published systems on 128 cores slower than one laptop thread on the same graphs, and the fastest only slightly quicker.', ['cost-hotos15']]
    ],
    explain: ['Explain how you decide whether to scale a component up or out.', ['Scale up: no code change, but a largest machine and one failure domain', 'Scale out needs stateless instances, or state partitioned across them', 'Coordination limits scale-out: a serial fraction caps speed-up; coherency makes throughput fall (USL)', 'Many small instances make failures cheaper and N + 2 headroom easy', 'Databases usually scale up first; compare with a single-machine baseline (COST) before distributing']],
    duck: ['Why not just keep buying bigger servers?', 'If ten servers are good, why aren\'t a thousand servers a hundred times better?'],
    lab: { type: 'estimate', brief: 'Size a tier and read a scalability curve. USL: C(N) = N ÷ (1 + α(N − 1) + βN(N − 1)); throughput peaks at N = √((1 − α) ÷ β).', fixed: [
      { q: 'Stack Overflow reported 504,816,843 SQL queries from HTTP requests on 9 February 2016. Average queries per second that day?', a: 5843, unit: 'queries/s', tol: 0.05, how: '504,816,843 ÷ 86,400 s ≈ 5,843 queries/s on average, with each SQL Server cluster\'s single primary taking almost all of its load. Peaks are higher.' },
      { q: 'α = 0.1, β = 0. Throughput of 10 nodes relative to one?', a: 5.3, unit: '×', tol: 0.05, how: '10 ÷ (1 + 0.1 × 9) = 10 ÷ 1.9 ≈ 5.3×. Half of the ten nodes\' capacity is lost to waiting on the serial part; the ceiling is 1/α = 10×.' },
      { q: 'α = 0.03, β = 0.0003. At about how many nodes does throughput peak?', a: 57, unit: 'nodes', tol: 0.05, how: '√((1 − 0.03) ÷ 0.0003) = √3,233 ≈ 57. Past that, the coherency cost grows faster than the added capacity and throughput falls.' },
      { q: 'Peak traffic needs 12 web instances. How many do you run to be N + 2?', a: 14, unit: 'instances', tol: 0, how: '12 + 2 = 14: peak is still served with one instance down for maintenance and another failed.' },
      { q: 'Instead, you run 2 large machines, each able to serve 60% of peak. With one down, what percentage of peak can you serve?', a: 60, unit: '%', tol: 0, how: 'One machine left, at 60% of peak: 40% of peak traffic is refused or queued. Few large failure domains make each failure expensive.' }
    ] }
  });

  T({
    id: '4.2', mod: '4', title: 'Autoscaling', tag: 'Capacity that follows load, minutes late',
    analogy: 'Calling in extra cooks for a dinner rush. A cook phoned at 7:00 arrives at 7:40 and needs ten minutes to set up, so a rush that is over by 7:30 is served by whoever was already on shift. A manager who knows Fridays are busy rosters the extra cooks in advance.',
    points: [
      'Target tracking works like a thermostat: choose a metric and a target, such as 50% average CPU, and the autoscaler sizes the group to hold it. Kubernetes HPA computes desired replicas = ceil(current replicas × current metric ÷ target), every 15 s by default, and ignores ratios within 10% of 1.0. Step scaling instead adds fixed increments when an alarm threshold is crossed.',
      'The metric must move in proportion to capacity: average CPU, requests per target, or queue backlog per instance. AWS lists total request count, latency and total queue length as metrics that do not work for target tracking, because adding instances does not move them proportionally. Topic 5.1 adds in-flight requests for GPU servers.',
      'Reaction time = metric delay + decision + provisioning + warm-up. EC2 metrics arrive every 5 minutes by default (1 minute with detailed monitoring), and a new instance does not count until its warm-up ends. A spike shorter than that sum is absorbed by capacity that was already running, or not at all.',
      'Scale out fast, scale in slowly. Kubernetes scales down only to the highest recommendation of the last 5 minutes, to stop flapping, while scale-up has no window; AWS blocks scale-in while new instances warm up. Removing capacity eagerly means paying the slow scale-out again at the next bump.',
      'Predictable load does not have to be detected. Scheduled actions change capacity at set times; AWS predictive scaling learns daily and weekly patterns from up to 14 days of history and launches ahead of the forecast, but never scales in by itself. Warm pools keep pre-initialised instances ready, which shortens the boot step.',
      'Autoscaling relies on a control plane and can deepen an outage. AWS overprovisions so a service survives losing an Availability Zone without launching anything (static stability). Google warns that when a dependency hangs, the autoscaler adds servers that pile more load onto it: set minimum and maximum bounds and keep a kill switch.'
    ],
    pitfall: 'Letting the autoscaler respond to a struggling dependency. The database slows, requests pile up on the web servers and hold their resources, the autoscaler adds servers, and every new server sends more queries to the database that needed relief. Bound the maximum by what the backend can absorb.',
    say: 'I would target-track requests per instance at about 60% of the load-tested maximum, with a minimum that survives losing one zone. Detection, boot and warm-up add up to about 4 minutes, so anything faster has to be absorbed by headroom; for the 9 a.m. ramp I would add scheduled or predictive scaling.',
    refs: [
      ['k8s-hpa', 'The HPA formula is a ratio — “From the most basic perspective, the HorizontalPodAutoscaler controller operates on the ratio between desired metric value and current metric value”'],
      ['k8s-hpa', 'Scale-down stabilisation — “The controller considers all recommendations within a configurable window choosing the highest recommendation from within that window.”'],
      ['aws-asg-target-tracking', 'A proportional metric — “The metric value must increase or decrease proportionally to the number of instances in the Auto Scaling group.”'],
      ['aws-asg-target-tracking', 'Metric delay — “By default, all Amazon EC2 metrics are published in five-minute intervals, but they are configurable to a lower interval of one minute by enabling detailed monitoring.”'],
      ['aws-asg-instance-warmup', 'Warm-up — “You can specify how long after an instance reaches the InService state it waits before contributing usage data to the aggregated metrics.”'],
      ['aws-asg-predictive-how', 'Predictive scaling only adds — “If the forecast expects a decrease in load, it will not scale in to remove capacity.”'],
      ['aws-asg-warm-pools', 'Warm pools — “A warm pool is a pool of pre-initialized EC2 instances that sits alongside an Auto Scaling group.”'],
      ['aws-bl-static-stability', 'Static stability — “a more effective, statically stable service would overprovision its infrastructure to the point where it would continue operating correctly without having to launch any new EC2 instances, even if an Availability Zone were to become impaired.”'],
      ['sre-workbook-managing-load', 'Reaction takes time — “Autoscaler also needs adequate time to react, particularly when new instances cannot turn up and serve instantly.”'],
      ['sre-workbook-managing-load', 'Scaling into a failing dependency — “Autoscaler will scale up the jobs, causing more and more traffic to get stuck. The increased load on your failing dependency can prevent the dependency from recovering.”']
    ],
    cards: [
      ['Why can autoscaling not absorb a 90-second spike?', 'Its reaction time is metric delay + decision + launch + boot + warm-up, typically several minutes. Anything shorter is carried by capacity already running, so headroom, scheduled or predictive scaling, or warm pools have to cover it.'],
      ['What makes a metric suitable for target tracking?', 'It must fall roughly in proportion as instances are added: average CPU, requests per target, backlog per instance. Totals such as all requests or all queued messages, and latency, do not, so the autoscaler cannot size the group from them.'],
      ['What is static stability, and why does it limit reliance on autoscaling?', 'Running enough capacity in advance that losing a zone needs no new launches. Recovery that depends on launching instances depends on a control plane, discovery and configuration services that are more likely to be impaired during the very incident.']
    ],
    quiz: [
      ['A Kubernetes HPA targets 60% average CPU. The deployment has 10 pods averaging 90% CPU. How many replicas does the next sync ask for?', ['15', '7', '13', '20'], 'ceil(10 × 90 ÷ 60) = 15. Seven inverts the ratio (10 × 60 ÷ 90, rounded up); 13 adds the 30-point overshoot as 30% more pods; 20 is the ceiling of the default scale-up policy for one 15-second period (it may at most double the pods), not the computed target.', ['k8s-hpa']],
      ['A flash sale lifts traffic from 50% to 140% of current capacity within 30 s and lasts 3 minutes. The group target-tracks CPU on 1-minute metrics, and a new instance needs about 4 minutes to launch, boot and warm up. What do users see?', ['Errors and queueing for most of the sale; new capacity lands after it ends', 'A short blip, since target tracking adds capacity within a single metric period', 'No effect, since the load balancer holds the requests until new instances are ready', 'A short delay while the 5-minute scale-up stabilisation window runs out first'], 'Detection needs at least a minute of data, then the instance needs about 4 minutes before it serves and counts: about 5 minutes against a 3-minute spike, so only the capacity already running helps. A fast decision does not help either: deciding to add an instance is not the same as having it serve.', ['aws-asg-target-tracking', 'aws-asg-instance-warmup', 'sre-workbook-managing-load']],
      ['Workers drain an SQS queue. Someone creates a target tracking policy on the queue\'s ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible with a target of 100. Why does AWS say this metric does not work, and what does?', ['The backlog does not fall in proportion to instances; track backlog per instance', 'Queue metrics are published too slowly to react; target-track the workers\' CPU instead', 'Target tracking needs a percentage metric; publish the queue as a percentage of capacity', 'Target tracking does not support SQS metrics; switch to a step scaling policy'], 'Target tracking assumes that doubling the instances roughly halves the metric. The total backlog does not behave that way, but a custom metric of messages per instance does. Percentages are not required (request count per target works), and CPU says little about workers that mostly wait on I/O.', ['aws-asg-target-tracking']],
      ['An HPA scales a deployment whose load swings between 50 and 70 req/s per pod every 2 minutes. With the scale-down stabilisation window set to 0, pods are added and removed constantly, and each new pod takes 90 s to warm up. What does restoring the default 300 s window do?', ['Scale-down uses the highest recommendation of the past 5 minutes, so pods stay', 'Scale-up also waits 5 minutes, so each peak is served by too few pods for a while', 'The metric is averaged over 5 minutes, so the HPA stops seeing the peaks', 'Pods are removed once they have served no traffic for a full 5 minutes'], 'The window keeps the highest recommendation seen in the last 5 minutes, so a 2-minute dip does not remove pods that the next peak will need. Scale-up has no stabilisation window by default, and the metric itself is not averaged by this setting.', ['k8s-hpa']],
      ['Checkout scales on in-flight requests per pod, and each pod holds a pool of 10 database connections. The payments database slows from 20 ms to 2 s per query, every pool is soon fully busy, and within minutes checkout grows from 20 pods to its maximum of 200. What has the autoscaler done to the incident?', ['Worsened it: up to 10× more connections and queries can now reach the database', 'Fixed it: in-flight requests per pod are back on target, so latency recovers', 'Raised the bill only: the extra pods sit idle and the database is unaffected', 'Shortened it: more pods drain the backlog of stuck requests more quickly'], 'Slower queries raise in-flight requests per pod (Little\'s Law), so the autoscaler adds pods, and each new pod brings its own pool of 10 connections: the database that is the bottleneck can now face up to 2,000 concurrent queries instead of 200. Scaling out spreads the waiting requests over more pods without making any query faster, so users still wait on the database, and a pool of 10 per pod caps one pod, not the fleet. A maximum bound sized to what the backend can take limits this.', ['sre-workbook-managing-load']]
    ],
    explain: ['Explain how you would configure autoscaling for a web service, and where autoscaling stops helping.', ['A metric that moves in proportion to capacity (CPU, requests per target, backlog per instance)', 'Reaction time = detection + launch + warm-up; shorter spikes need headroom', 'Scale out fast, scale in slowly: stabilisation and warm-up stop flapping', 'Scheduled or predictive scaling and warm pools for known or slow ramps', 'Bounds, static stability across zones, and the dependency that cannot scale']],
    duck: ['If the autoscaler adds servers whenever we are busy, why pay for spare ones?', 'Why not scale down as quickly as we scale up and save the money?'],
    lab: { type: 'estimate', brief: 'Tune an autoscaler and the headroom around it.', fixed: [
      { q: 'An HPA targets 50% CPU. Eight pods average 75%. Desired replicas?', a: 12, unit: 'pods', tol: 0, how: 'ceil(8 × 75 ÷ 50) = ceil(12) = 12.' },
      { q: 'Detecting a spike takes 2 minutes, launching an instance 90 s, and booting and warming the application 120 s. Seconds from the spike until new capacity serves?', a: 330, unit: 'seconds', tol: 0.05, how: '120 + 90 + 120 = 330 s, five and a half minutes. Every spike shorter than this is carried by what is already running.' },
      { q: 'Steady load is 600 req/s, each instance tops out at 100 req/s, and target tracking holds them at 60%. By what percentage can load jump before the running fleet is at 100%?', a: 67, unit: '%', tol: 0.05, how: '600 ÷ (100 × 0.6) = 10 instances = 1,000 req/s of capacity. (1,000 − 600) ÷ 600 ≈ 67%. The target value is your spike budget.' },
      { q: 'Peak needs the capacity of 12 instances. They are spread evenly over 3 zones, and losing any one zone must need no new launches. How many instances do you run?', a: 18, unit: 'instances', tol: 0, how: 'The two surviving zones must hold 12, so 6 per zone × 3 zones = 18: 50% overprovisioning, the figure AWS gives for three zones.' }
    ] }
  });

  T({
    id: '4.3', mod: '4', title: 'CDN and edge', tag: 'Shared copies near users, keyed carefully',
    analogy: 'A chain of newsagents stocking the morning paper. Every shop holds copies, so readers do not travel to the printer, but a letter addressed to one person never goes on the shelf. When a story changes, printing a new edition with a new date is easier than recalling every copy.',
    points: [
      'A CDN is a shared HTTP cache in many locations. The cache key is at minimum the method and URL; CDNs add more (Cloudflare\'s default adds the scheme, host, query string and the request\'s Origin header). Every value added to the key splits one object into several copies and lowers the hit ratio.',
      'The origin sets the rules with Cache-Control. max-age applies to every cache; s-maxage overrides it for shared caches such as a CDN; private forbids shared caches to store the response; no-store forbids every cache. stale-while-revalidate lets a cache serve a stale copy while it refreshes in the background, so max-age=600 with stale-while-revalidate=600 can serve a copy up to 20 minutes old.',
      'Three ways to change content: wait for the TTL, purge (by path, or by a tag attached to many objects), or publish under a new name. CloudFront recommends versioned file names for files that change often, because browsers and corporate proxies holding the old copy are bypassed too, and nothing has to be invalidated.',
      'Origin load = edge requests × miss ratio, so raising the hit ratio from 95% to 99% cuts origin traffic five times. A tiered cache or origin shield adds a layer between the edges and the origin: a miss at one of hundreds of edges is answered by that layer, and simultaneous misses for one object collapse into as few as one origin request.',
      'Personalised and authorised responses do not belong in a shared cache. A shared cache may not reuse a response to a request with an Authorization header unless the response explicitly allows it. Valve reported that on 25 December 2015 a caching configuration error meant store pages for about 34,000 Steam users may have been shown to other users.',
      'Edge compute runs small functions in the CDN\'s locations: redirects, header rewrites, A/B assignment, token checks. Limits are tight (a Cloudflare Workers isolate may use up to 128 MB of memory), and anything that needs the origin\'s data still makes the trip to the origin.'
    ],
    pitfall: 'Caching at the edge a page that varies by user, without putting the user in the cache key or marking it private. The first visitor\'s version is served to everyone after them; in Valve\'s 2015 incident, pages that can include billing addresses and purchase histories may have been shown to other users this way.',
    say: 'Static assets get content-hashed file names and a max-age of a year; public HTML gets a short max-age with stale-while-revalidate; anything behind login is Cache-Control: private. At a 98% hit ratio with an origin shield, 50,000 req/s at the edge is about 1,000 req/s at the origin.',
    refs: [
      ['rfc9111', 'The cache key — “The "cache key" is the information a cache uses to choose a response and is composed from, at a minimum, the request method and target URI used to retrieve the stored response”'],
      ['cloudflare-cache-keys', 'A bigger key costs hit rate — “They provide the benefit of more control, though they may reduce your cache hit rate and result in cache sharding”'],
      ['cloudflare-cache-keys', 'Cookies enter the key only through a custom key — “Like query_string or header, cookie controls which cookies appear in the Cache Key.”'],
      ['cloudflare-default-cache', 'No HTML by default — “The Cloudflare CDN does not cache HTML or JSON by default.”'],
      ['rfc9111', 's-maxage — “The s-maxage response directive indicates that, for a shared cache, the maximum age specified by this directive overrides the maximum age specified by either the max-age directive or the Expires header field.”'],
      ['rfc9111', 'private — “The unqualified private response directive indicates that a shared cache MUST NOT store the response (i.e., the response is intended for a single user).”'],
      ['rfc5861', 'stale-while-revalidate adds to max-age — “For example, with both set to 600, the server must be able to tolerate the response being served from cache for up to 20 minutes.”'],
      ['cloudfront-invalidation', 'Why versioned names beat invalidation — “If you invalidate the file, the user might continue to see the old version until it expires from those caches.”'],
      ['cloudflare-tiered-cache', 'Tiered cache — “If the upper-tier does not have the content, only the upper-tier can ask the origin for content.”'],
      ['cloudfront-origin-shield', 'Collapsed misses — “Requests for content that is not in Origin Shield’s cache are consolidated with other requests for the same object, resulting in as few as one request going to your origin.”'],
      ['valve-steam-christmas-2015', 'Caching authenticated pages, 2015 — “During the second wave of this attack, a second caching configuration was deployed that incorrectly cached web traffic for authenticated users.”'],
      ['cloudflare-workers-limits', 'Edge compute is small — “Each isolate can consume up to 128 MB of memory, including the JavaScript heap and WebAssembly allocations.”']
    ],
    cards: [
      ['What is in a CDN cache key, and why does adding to it cost you?', 'At minimum the method and URL; CDNs add the host, scheme, query string and some headers. Each extra value (a cookie, a header, a tracking parameter) splits one object into many copies, so each copy is requested less and the hit ratio falls.'],
      ['max-age, s-maxage, private, no-store: what does each do?', 'max-age: freshness lifetime for any cache. s-maxage: overrides it for shared caches such as a CDN. private: shared caches must not store it; the browser may. no-store: no cache of any kind may store it.'],
      ['Why prefer versioned file names to purges for static assets?', 'A new name is a new cache key, so every cache, including browsers and corporate proxies the CDN cannot purge, fetches the new file at once, and old and new pages can each reference their own version. It also costs no invalidation requests.']
    ],
    quiz: [
      ['A CDN serves 50,000 req/s with a 96% hit ratio. Narrowing the cache key raises it to 99%. Requests reaching the origin go from…', ['2,000/s to 500/s', '2,000/s to 1,940/s', '2,000/s to 1,500/s', '48,000/s to 49,500/s'], 'Origin traffic is the misses: 4% of 50,000 = 2,000, and 1% = 500, a fourfold cut from three points of hit ratio. 1,940 treats the 3 points as a 3% reduction; 1,500 subtracts the new rate from the old instead of replacing it; 48,000 and 49,500 are the hits.', 'calc'],
      ['A product page greets the user by name and shows their cart count; the response sets no cookie. The site is on Cloudflare with the default cache key and a Cache Rule that makes the page eligible for cache and respects origin Cache-Control. For a spike, the team adds Cache-Control: public, s-maxage=60. What happens?', ['Visitors get whichever user\'s page that edge cached, for up to 60 s', 'Just the browsers cache it, as shared caches ignore s-maxage', 'Cloudflare keys on the session cookie, so each user gets their own copy', 'Cloudflare keys on the client IP, so each user still sees their own page'], 's-maxage tells shared caches they may store and reuse the response, and the Cache Rule lets Cloudflare cache this HTML at all (by default it does not cache HTML). Cloudflare\'s default key is the URL plus a few headers such as Origin, not cookies or the client address, so the first response cached at each edge is served to everyone. Valve\'s 2015 incident was the same class of error: a caching configuration that stored authenticated users\' pages in a shared cache.', ['rfc9111', 'cloudflare-cache-keys', 'cloudflare-default-cache', 'valve-steam-christmas-2015']],
      ['app.js is served with a max-age of one year. A bug fix must reach users today. Which approach does CloudFront recommend for files that change often, and why?', ['Rename it, e.g. app.3f9c2b.js, so copies cached anywhere are bypassed', 'Invalidate /app.js at the CDN, so caches fetch the new version next time', 'Set max-age=60 on app.js from now on, so the fix spreads in a minute', 'Purge the whole distribution, so that each edge refetches its assets'], 'An invalidation clears CloudFront\'s caches, but browsers and corporate proxies keep the old file until it expires, here for a year. A new file name is a new cache key everywhere, once the HTML that references it points to the new name. Changing max-age only affects copies fetched after the change.', ['cloudfront-invalidation']],
      ['Prices may be at most 2 minutes stale. To hide origin latency, a colleague sends Cache-Control: max-age=120, stale-while-revalidate=120. Does this meet the limit?', ['No: one fetched copy can be served for up to 4 minutes', 'Yes: the stale window applies just while the origin returns errors', 'Yes: the background refresh means no reader receives a stale copy', 'No: stale copies may then be served indefinitely until a purge'], 'The longest a copy can be served is max-age plus the stale-while-revalidate window: 120 + 120 = 240 s. The request that triggers the background refresh is itself answered with the stale copy. Serving stale only on errors is stale-if-error, a different directive.', ['rfc5861']],
      ['Thumbnails come from one origin in Virginia behind a CDN with hundreds of edge locations. When a new episode launches, first requests from every edge reach the origin at once and overload it, although the steady hit ratio is 97%. Which change targets this?', ['An origin shield or tiered cache, so edges miss to one shared layer', 'A longer TTL, so thumbnails stay in the edge caches for more days', 'More edge locations, so each serves fewer users and fewer requests', 'Cache-Control: private, so that browsers cache the thumbnails instead'], 'Each edge misses once per new object, so hundreds of edges mean hundreds of origin fetches. A shield or upper tier answers those misses itself and collapses concurrent misses for an object into one origin request. A longer TTL does nothing for the first fetch, and more edges mean more first misses.', ['cloudfront-origin-shield', 'cloudflare-tiered-cache']]
    ],
    explain: ['Explain what you would cache at the CDN for a web application, and how you would keep it correct.', ['The cache key, and why each extra value lowers the hit ratio', 'Cache-Control: max-age, s-maxage, private, no-store, stale-while-revalidate', 'Versioned URLs for assets; TTL or purge for the rest', 'Never cache personalised or authorised responses in a shared cache', 'Origin load = requests × miss ratio; an origin shield or tiered cache']],
    duck: ['If caching makes pages fast, why not cache every page at the edge?', 'If the CDN keeps a file for a year, how does anyone get the new version?'],
    lab: { type: 'sort', brief: 'How should each response be cached?', buckets: ['Edge, long TTL (immutable)', 'Edge, short TTL', 'Never in a shared cache'], items: [
      ['app.3f9c2b.js, a bundle whose name contains its content hash', 0], ['A font file whose URL includes its version number', 0], ['Segment 000123.ts of a film that will never be re-encoded', 0], ['logo.9a1e.svg, referenced from every page', 0],
      ['Home page HTML that is the same for every visitor', 1], ['Public list of top headlines, updated every minute', 1], ['A city\'s weather forecast, refreshed every 10 minutes', 1], ['Public price list for a sale, changed a few times an hour', 1],
      ['A logged-in user\'s order history page', 2], ['Profile JSON returned for a request with a user\'s bearer token', 2], ['Cart page listing the items this user has added', 2], ['Response to the POST that places an order', 2]
    ] }
  });

  T({
    id: '4.4', mod: '4', title: 'Multi-region', tag: 'Surviving the loss of a region',
    analogy: 'A firm with offices in two cities. A second office with copies of the files keeps work going if one city floods, but files copied each evening miss that day\'s work, and if both offices edit the same contract the copies disagree. A fully staffed second office costs almost as much as the first.',
    points: [
      'AWS names four disaster-recovery strategies, cheapest and slowest first: backup and restore, pilot light (data replicated, servers off), warm standby (a scaled-down copy that can take traffic at once) and multi-site active/active. Choose by RTO and RPO (topic 0D.2), and by what the business will pay.',
      'Asynchronous cross-region replication keeps writes fast and loses the replication lag on a regional failure; Aurora Global Database typically replicates in under a second, and DynamoDB global tables in their default (MREC) mode within a second or less. Synchronous replication loses nothing, but every write waits for another region: about 45 ms round trip within the US, 70 ms New York to London.',
      'Decide where each write goes. Write global sends every write to one region (Aurora: one writer, read-only secondary regions). Write local accepts writes everywhere and needs a conflict rule: DynamoDB global tables in their default (MREC) mode keep the later write per item and drop the other. Write partitioned homes each user in one region so their writes cannot conflict.',
      'Staying writable through a whole-region loss with a consensus database needs at least three regions: CockroachDB then keeps 5 replicas across 3 regions, and every write pays at least one round trip to the nearest other region. Data residency can override all of this: CockroachDB super regions keep chosen data only in member regions.',
      'Traffic moves by DNS, anycast or a global load balancer. DNS failover takes at least detection plus the record\'s TTL, because resolvers cache answers and not all of them honour the TTL. Anycast addresses avoid DNS caching: a standard AWS Global Accelerator\'s static addresses stay the same while it sends traffic only to healthy endpoints.',
      'Failover needs capacity that already exists. With N regions sharing load evenly, losing one adds 1/(N−1) more load to each survivor, so each must normally run below (N−1)/N of its maximum. Scaling up during the disaster depends on a control plane. AWS says a DR strategy must be regularly assessed and tested.'
    ],
    pitfall: 'An active-passive setup whose passive region is never exercised. On the day it is needed the standby is too small, quotas block scaling it up, its configuration has drifted, and DNS keeps sending clients to the dead region for the length of the TTL.',
    say: 'I would run active-active in three regions at no more than 60% each, so losing one puts the survivors at 90%. Each user\'s writes go to a home region and replicate asynchronously in about a second, so a regional loss can drop about a second of writes; money movement goes to a strongly consistent store and pays a cross-region round trip per write.',
    refs: [
      ['aws-dr-options', 'Four strategies — “Disaster recovery strategies available to you within AWS can be broadly categorized into four approaches, ranging from the low cost and low complexity of making backups to more complex strategies using multiple active Regions.”'],
      ['aws-dr-options', 'Pilot light vs warm standby — “The distinction is that pilot light cannot process requests without additional action taken first, whereas warm standby can handle traffic (at reduced capacity levels) immediately.”'],
      ['aws-dr-options', 'Scaling up is a control-plane dependency — “Because Auto Scaling is a control plane activity, taking a dependency on it will lower the resiliency of your overall recovery strategy.”'],
      ['aws-dr-options', 'Write partitioned — “A write partitioned strategy assigns writes to a specific Region based on a partition key (like user ID) to avoid write conflicts.”'],
      ['aurora-global-database', 'Asynchronous, under a second — “After any write operation, Aurora replicates data to the secondary AWS Regions using dedicated infrastructure, with latency typically under a second.”'],
      ['dynamodb-global-tables-how', 'MREC, the default mode, replicates asynchronously — “Item changes in an MREC global table replica are asynchronously replicated to all other replicas, typically within a second or less.”'],
      ['dynamodb-global-tables-how', 'Last writer wins (MREC mode) — “If the same item is modified in multiple Regions simultaneously, DynamoDB will resolve the conflict by using the modification with the latest internal timestamp on a per-item basis, referred to as a "last writer wins" conflict resolution method.”'],
      ['sre-managing-critical-state', 'Cross-region round trips — “A typical round-trip-time (RTT) within the United States is 45 milliseconds, and from New York to London is 70 milliseconds.”'],
      ['crdb-survival-goals', 'The price of surviving a region — “This added survival comes at a cost: write latency will be increased by at least as much as the round-trip time to the nearest region.”'],
      ['crdb-multiregion-overview', 'Data residency — “The primary use case for super regions is data domiciling. Data from regional and regional by row tables is stored only in regions that are members of the super region.”'],
      ['sre-lb-frontend', 'DNS TTL bounds failover — “Given that authoritative nameservers cannot flush resolvers’ caches, DNS records need a relatively low TTL.”'],
      ['sre-lb-frontend', 'Some resolvers ignore the TTL — “Sadly, not all DNS resolvers respect the TTL value set by authoritative nameservers.”'],
      ['aws-global-accelerator-how', 'Anycast static addresses — “The static IP addresses are anycast from the AWS edge network.”'],
      ['aws-global-accelerator-how', 'Traffic only to healthy endpoints — “For standard accelerators, AWS Global Accelerator automatically checks the health of the endpoints that are associated with your static IP addresses, and then directs user traffic only to healthy endpoints.”'],
      ['aws-bl-static-stability', '(N−1)/N headroom, as AWS applies it to three zones — “Put another way, we overprovision such that each Availability Zone is operating at only 66 percent of the level for which we have load-tested it.”']
    ],
    cards: [
      ['Name the four AWS disaster-recovery strategies, from cheapest to fastest recovery.', 'Backup and restore; pilot light (data replicated, servers off until needed); warm standby (a scaled-down copy already serving or able to serve); multi-site active/active (every region takes traffic). Cost and complexity rise as RTO and RPO fall.'],
      ['Write global, write local, write partitioned: what does each mean?', 'Write global: every write goes to one primary region, which is promoted elsewhere on failure. Write local: each region accepts writes and conflicts are resolved, e.g. last writer wins. Write partitioned: each key, such as a user, has a home region for its writes, so conflicts cannot occur.'],
      ['How much headroom does each of N active regions need to survive losing one?', 'Each region normally runs at most (N−1)/N of its maximum: 50% with two regions, 67% with three, 75% with four. The survivors absorb the lost region\'s share without launching anything.']
    ],
    quiz: [
      ['A payments service in New York commits each write locally in 5 ms, then waits for a synchronous replica in London to confirm it (round trip about 70 ms). A checkout makes 4 writes, one after another. Minimum time spent on writes per checkout?', ['About 300 ms', 'About 160 ms', 'About 75 ms', 'About 20 ms'], 'Each write costs 5 + 70 = 75 ms, and four sequential writes cost 300 ms. 160 ms uses the one-way time (35 ms) instead of the round trip; 75 ms counts one write; 20 ms ignores the replica.', ['sre-managing-critical-state']],
      ['A shared shopping list uses DynamoDB global tables in the default multi-Region eventual consistency mode, and users write to their nearest Region. Two people in different Regions update the same item within the same second: one ticks it off, the other changes the quantity. What ends up stored?', ['The edit with the later timestamp; the other is lost', 'Both edits, merged attribute by attribute into one item', 'The first edit; the second fails with a conditional-check error', 'Both versions side by side, for the application to reconcile'], 'Conflicts are resolved per item by the latest internal timestamp, and every replica converges on that version, so one person\'s change silently disappears. Fields are not merged, and no sibling versions are kept: designs that cannot lose either edit route both writes to one Region or partition writes by key.', ['dynamodb-global-tables-how']],
      ['Your recovery Region holds continuously replicated databases and an Auto Scaling group with desired capacity 0. The primary Region fails. What must happen before users are served there, and what is the risk?', ['Servers must be launched and scaled up, a control-plane step during the outage', 'Nothing: it serves at reduced capacity at once, then scales up to full size', 'Data must first be restored from snapshots, which takes several hours', 'Only the DNS record must change, so its TTL is the whole recovery time'], 'This is pilot light: the data is there but nothing can serve until servers are started and scaled out, and AWS warns that relying on Auto Scaling, a control-plane activity, lowers the resilience of the recovery. Serving at reduced capacity immediately describes warm standby.', ['aws-dr-options']],
      ['A CockroachDB database runs in us-east and us-west, and must keep accepting reads and writes if either Region is lost. What does it need?', ['A third Region, since surviving a Region failure needs at least three', 'Five replicas, three in us-east and two in us-west, so a majority survives', 'Synchronous replication between the two, so that either can take over', 'Asynchronous replication, with automatic promotion of the us-west copy'], 'Writes need a majority of replicas. With two Regions, one of them always holds the majority, and losing it stops writes; three Regions with replicas 2 + 2 + 1 keep a majority after any single loss. Asynchronous promotion keeps the database writable but can lose acknowledged writes and diverge.', ['crdb-survival-goals']],
      ['DNS failover uses health checks every 30 s with a failure threshold of 3, and the record\'s TTL is 300 s. The primary Region dies. Roughly how long until every client whose resolver honours the TTL has moved?', ['About 6.5 minutes', 'About 1.5 minutes', 'About 5 minutes', 'About 30 seconds'], 'About 3 × 30 = 90 s to declare the endpoint unhealthy, then up to 300 s for cached answers to expire: 390 s. 1.5 minutes forgets the cache; 5 minutes forgets detection. Resolvers that ignore TTLs take longer still, which is one reason to keep failover TTLs low or use anycast.', ['sre-lb-frontend']]
    ],
    explain: ['Explain how you would make a service survive the loss of a whole region.', ['Choose a DR strategy (backup, pilot light, warm standby, active/active) from RTO and RPO', 'Asynchronous replication loses the lag; synchronous pays a cross-region round trip per write', 'Where writes go: global, local with conflict resolution, or partitioned by key', 'Traffic failover by DNS (detection + TTL) or anycast', 'Capacity in the survivors: each region below (N−1)/N; test the failover']],
    duck: ['Why not write to every region at once so nothing is ever lost?', 'If we already have a second region, why does failing over take minutes?'],
    lab: { type: 'estimate', brief: 'Size a multi-region deployment and its failover.', fixed: [
      { q: 'Traffic is spread evenly over 3 active regions. Highest normal utilisation per region so that losing one needs no new capacity?', a: 66.7, unit: '%', tol: 0.03, how: 'The two survivors carry all the load: (N−1)/N = 2/3 ≈ 66.7%.' },
      { q: 'Peak is 30,000 req/s and one region can serve at most 12,000 req/s. How many active regions survive the loss of any one?', a: 4, unit: 'regions', tol: 0, how: 'Survivors must hold 30,000: 30,000 ÷ 12,000 = 2.5, so 3 survivors, plus the one that fails = 4.' },
      { q: 'Asynchronous replication lags 800 ms behind and the region takes 5,000 writes/s. Roughly how many acknowledged writes are lost if the region vanishes?', a: 4000, unit: 'writes', tol: 0.05, how: '5,000 × 0.8 s = 4,000 writes that the primary acknowledged and no other region has.' },
      { q: 'A write must be acknowledged by a replica in another region 70 ms away (round trip) and takes 3 ms locally. Minimum write latency?', a: 73, unit: 'ms', tol: 0.05, how: '3 + 70 = 73 ms, against about 3 ms for a write that only needs replicas in the same region.' }
    ] }
  });

  T({
    id: '4.5', mod: '4', title: 'Microservices', tag: 'Split by team and data ownership',
    analogy: 'One restaurant kitchen against a food court. Separate stalls can change menus and hire staff without asking each other, but a meal with dishes from five stalls means five queues, and if one stall is closed the tray is incomplete. A small restaurant with one cook gains nothing by becoming a food court.',
    points: [
      'A microservice is deployed independently and owns one business capability. The split buys independent releases, ownership that follows team lines (Conway\'s law) and the option to scale one part separately. A monolith scales too, by running many copies behind a load balancer; what it cannot do is scale one part alone.',
      'It costs distribution. Remote calls are slow and can fail, so a request that calls six services that each call six more adds up their latencies; data spread over services makes cross-service updates eventually consistent; and many more things have to be deployed, monitored and paged on.',
      'Draw boundaries around data. Each service keeps its data private and reachable only through its API (database per service), which can be private tables or a schema on a shared server. Joins and transactions across services then become API composition, replicated views (CQRS) and sagas.',
      'The distributed monolith: services that share tables or must be released together pay every cost of distribution and get no independence. Fowler: a suite of services that must coordinate its deployments is not a microservice architecture.',
      'A service mesh moves network behaviour out of the code into a proxy beside each instance (a sidecar such as Envoy, which Istio uses; Linkerd has its own proxy): mTLS, retries, timeouts, traffic shifting for canaries and uniform telemetry, in any language. It adds a proxy on each side of every call, CPU and memory per pod (about 0.2 vCPU and 60 MB per 1,000 req/s for an Istio 1.24 sidecar), and a control plane to operate.',
      'Start with a monolith. Fowler observed that almost every successful microservice system began as a monolith that grew and was split, partly because boundaries are hard to draw before the domain is understood. Segment wrote in 2018 that it merged over 140 services into one; Prime Video wrote in 2023 that moving one tool into a single process cut its infrastructure cost by over 90%.'
    ],
    pitfall: 'Splitting a young product into many services by technical layer or by noun, over a shared database. Every feature touches several services that must be released together, and each user request crosses the network many times.',
    say: 'I would start as a modular monolith and pull a module out when a team needs its own release schedule or the module needs different hardware, and only along a boundary where it can own its data. With dozens of services in several languages, a mesh gives mTLS, timeouts and canary traffic shifting without code changes, at the cost of two sidecar hops on every call.',
    refs: [
      ['fowler-lewis-microservices', 'What a monolith cannot scale — “Scaling requires scaling of the entire application rather than parts of it that require greater resource.”'],
      ['fowler-microservice-tradeoffs', 'The cost of distribution — “Distributed systems are harder to program, since remote calls are slow and are always at risk of failure.”'],
      ['fowler-microservice-tradeoffs', 'The distributed monolith — “So it\'s reasonable to say that a suite of services that must have its deployments coordinated is not a microservice architecture.”'],
      ['fowler-monolith-first', 'Monolith first — “Almost all the successful microservice stories have started with a monolith that got too big and was broken up”'],
      ['fowler-monolith-first', 'Boundaries are hard to draw early — “But even experienced architects working in familiar domains have great difficulty getting boundaries right at the beginning.”'],
      ['fowler-monolith-first', 'A monolith built to be split — “The logical way is to design a monolith carefully, paying attention to modularity within the software, both at the API boundaries and how the data is stored.”'],
      ['microservices-io-database-per-service', 'Database per service — “Keep each microservice’s persistent data private to that service and accessible only via its API.”'],
      ['envoy-what-is', 'The sidecar — “Envoy is a self contained process that is designed to run alongside every application server.”'],
      ['linkerd-service-mesh', 'What a mesh does — “For example, Linkerd uses a wide array of powerful techniques such as mutual TLS (mTLS), latency-aware load balancing, retries, success rate instrumentation, transparent traffic shifting, and more.”'],
      ['istio-performance', 'What a sidecar costs — “In Istio 1.24, with 1000 http requests per second containing 1 KB of payload each a single sidecar proxy with 2 worker threads consumes about 0.20 vCPU and 60 MB of memory.”'],
      ['segment-goodbye-microservices', 'Segment, 2018 — “The first item on the list was to consolidate the now over 140 services into a single service.”'],
      ['primevideo-monolith-2023', 'Prime Video, 2023 — “To address this, we moved all components into a single process to keep the data transfer within the process memory, which also simplified the orchestration logic.”']
    ],
    cards: [
      ['What does splitting into microservices buy, and what does it cost?', 'Buys: independent deployment, team ownership, separate scaling and technology choice per service. Costs: slow and failing remote calls, eventual consistency across services, and much more to deploy, monitor and operate.'],
      ['What is a distributed monolith?', 'Services that share a database or must be released together. They pay the network and operational costs of microservices without gaining independent deployment.'],
      ['What does a sidecar proxy take out of application code, and what does it add?', 'Takes out: mTLS, retries, timeouts, load balancing, traffic shifting and telemetry, implemented once for every language. Adds: a proxy hop on each side of every call, CPU and memory per pod, and a control plane to run and upgrade.']
    ],
    quiz: [
      ['A page handler calls 6 services one after another. Each call spends 15 ms in the callee and passes through two sidecar proxies (the caller\'s and the callee\'s), each adding 1 ms per call, request and response included. Ignoring network time, what is the minimum time for the six calls?', ['102 ms', '90 ms', '96 ms', '17 ms'], '6 × (15 + 2) = 102 ms. 90 ms leaves out the sidecars; 96 ms counts one sidecar per call; 17 ms is a single call, which is roughly what six parallel calls would cost.', 'calc'],
      ['Twelve services share one Postgres schema. Adding a column to the orders table means releasing five of them together, in a fixed order, on the same night. What is this, and what is the fix?', ['A distributed monolith; give each table one owning service and an API', 'Normal coupling for microservices; add a service mesh to sequence the releases', 'Write contention on one table; shard orders by customer to spread the load', 'Version drift; keep the twelve services in a single repository'], 'Services that share tables cannot change them independently, so their releases have to be coordinated, which Fowler says means it is not a microservice architecture. Database per service makes each table private to one service. A mesh manages traffic, not schemas.', ['fowler-microservice-tradeoffs', 'microservices-io-database-per-service']],
      ['A four-engineer startup is building its first product and expects to grow. The CTO proposes 15 microservices from day one to avoid a rewrite later. What does Fowler\'s "Monolith First" argue?', ['Build a monolith first; service boundaries are hard to get right this early', 'Build a monolith first; in-process calls let it scale further than services can', 'Build a monolith first; one codebase lets the team skip module boundaries for now', 'Build a monolith first; one shared database means no data migration when it splits'], 'Fowler reports that successful microservice systems usually started as a monolith that was split, and that even experienced architects struggle to draw stable boundaries at the start; moving a feature between services is much harder than inside a monolith. Scale is not his argument, and he calls designing the monolith carefully for modularity the logical way to do it, at its API boundaries and in how data is stored, so that it can be split later.', ['fowler-monolith-first']],
      ['Forty services in Go, Java and Python each implement retries, timeouts and TLS with different libraries, and certificates are rotated by hand. A sidecar service mesh is proposed. What does it change, and at what cost?', ['A proxy per pod does mTLS, retries and timeouts; each call crosses two proxies', 'The proxies cache responses between services; each pod needs more memory for it', 'Retries move into one shared client library; each team has to adopt it in its code', 'Services merge into one process; a crash in one now takes the others down'], 'The sidecar is a separate process next to each instance, so the behaviour is implemented once for every language and upgraded without touching the services. The price is an extra proxy on each side of every call, its CPU and memory, and a control plane. A mesh proxy forwards calls and, by default, does not cache their responses; a shared library is the approach the mesh replaces, and it has to exist in every language.', ['envoy-what-is', 'linkerd-service-mesh', 'istio-performance']],
      ['Prime Video\'s stream-monitoring tool (2023) ran each step as a separate serverless component, passed every video frame between them through S3, and drove per-second state transitions with Step Functions. It hit a scaling limit at about 5% of expected load. Which change fits the cause?', ['Run all components in one process and pass the frames in memory', 'Split the detectors into smaller functions so each scales alone', 'Add a service mesh so calls between components are retried', 'Shard the S3 bucket by stream so uploads spread over more prefixes'], 'The cost and the limit came from moving data and control between components, so removing the boundaries removed both; the team reported cutting infrastructure cost by over 90%. Smaller functions would add more hand-offs, which is the problem itself.', ['primevideo-monolith-2023']]
    ],
    explain: ['Explain how you would decide whether, and where, to split a system into microservices.', ['What a split buys: independent deployment, team ownership, separate scaling', 'What it costs: remote calls, partial failure, eventual consistency, operations', 'Boundaries around data ownership; avoid the distributed monolith', 'Service mesh and sidecar: what they take out of the code and what they add', 'Monolith first; split when a real team or scaling need appears']],
    duck: ['Why not make every class its own service, so teams never get in each other\'s way?', 'If a service mesh handles retries and security, why would anyone not use one?'],
    lab: { type: 'pick', brief: 'Pick the four reasons that justify moving a module into its own service.', want: 4, options: [
      ['Its team must release several times a day without waiting for others', true, 'Independent deployment is the main thing a split buys.'],
      ['It needs GPUs while the rest of the system runs on small CPU hosts', true, 'Different hardware for one module is something a monolith cannot give.'],
      ['It must run on 50 instances at peak while the rest needs only 3', true, 'A monolith scales only as a whole, so it would run 50 copies of everything.'],
      ['A crash in it must never take down checkout with it', true, 'A separate process isolates its failures; in one process a crash takes everything down.'],
      ['Its code would be easier to read if it lived in its own repository', false, 'Readable modules are possible inside a monolith; a repository is not a service boundary.'],
      ['It shares five tables with orders and changes in the same releases', false, 'That makes a distributed monolith: coordinated releases with network calls added.'],
      ['The platform team would like a first service to try the mesh on', false, 'Tooling curiosity is not a reason to take on the costs of distribution.'],
      ['Orders calls it in a loop, several hundred times per request', false, 'A chatty boundary turns in-process calls into hundreds of slow, fallible remote calls.']
    ] }
  });
})(SD.T);
